From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: katamaran <katamaran@localhost>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 22:10:29 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 11/12] dns: cache upstream answers and coalesce identical
 lookups

Every query used to go upstream and every answer went out with a TTL of
0, so clients could not cache either. Add an answer cache, keyed by name
(case-insensitively), type and class, that keeps an answer for its
smallest TTL (between 2 s and 10 min), a negative one for its SOA's
TTL capped by the SOA MINIMUM (RFC 2308) or the source's negative TTL
(between 1 s and 5 min), holds at most 4096 answers (least recently
used out first), and never keeps failures. Answers go out with their
TTLs counted down and capped at how long the cache still keeps them.
Concurrent askers of the same question share one upstream lookup,
which an asker giving up does not cancel. A watch on the host's DNS
configuration can empty the cache, and lookups in flight across such a
change are not stored.

The handler asks a resolver built on this cache, when it has one, for
every name no local zone owns, falling back to the existing paths if
the resolver is unavailable. Answers carry the owner name as the client
spelled it. For sources that cannot tell NXDOMAIN from NODATA, the
resolver probes the name's other address types through the cache: a
name with an A or AAAA record exists. That keeps AAAA for an
IPv4-only name NODATA, which musl needs (an NXDOMAIN there fails the
whole getaddrinfo), and the probe reuses the A answer the client asks
for alongside.
